**Read:** [Polymer Physics — MIT 3.063](../sources/Polymer-Physics-MIT-3-063.lingua#r_5EPM1GX0YZDKN71N771PQCWQ5P), lectures 1–8 and 17–19; [Materials structure and observation — Cambridge DoITPoMS](../sources/Materials-Structure-and-Observation-DoITPoMS.lingua#r_57315283EDNWKYC1PQE23G2AKF), Polymer Basics and Glass Transition; [Material families and mechanical response — Cambridge DoITPoMS](../sources/Material-Families-and-Mechanical-Response-DoITPoMS.lingua#r_79PNN694CQ82ENKZC6STQJ5KN9), Crystallinity in Polymers, including the polyethylene tensile example. Work the corresponding public MIT assignments.

Chain chemistry, molecular-mass distribution, branching, tacticity, entanglements, crystallinity and orientation all affect response. Thermoplastic behavior concerns a material's ability to soften and flow under appropriate conditions; it does not guarantee unlimited reheating without chemical damage. Distinguish melting of crystalline regions from the glass transition of an amorphous fraction.

In a semicrystalline polymer, lamellae, tie chains and amorphous regions cooperate. Necking and drawing can reorganize that structure. A constant crosshead speed does not ensure a spatially uniform strain rate once deformation localizes. Fillers, plasticizers, absorbed water and prior processing can alter the observed response.

**Work:** Compare polyethylene, a nylon and an amorphous thermoplastic using grade-specific evidence. Explain why a transparent/opaque appearance is an incomplete crystallinity measurement. Reconcile calorimetry, diffraction and mechanical observations rather than assigning every change to one scalar crystallinity value.

**Return:** [Materials engineering — structure, processing and useful behavior](../Materials-engineering.lingua#r_05BF5JM8141RKF1VT5998YFZQ9).
